eier","locid":"9504265d-0046-4e58-b529-256a656c012d","name":"Hardmeier, Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-6103-7275","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-63d5d848-3210-4249-af8f-6e81ff8ca8c5"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"SafeNet: Challenges and strategies in combatting online hate speech","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718860","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"This research quantitatively investigates the impact of violence on the propagation of images in social media in the context of political protest. Using a computational approach, we measure the relative violence of a large set of images shared on Twitter during the protests against the G20 summit in Frankfurt am Main in 2017. This allows us to investigate if more violent content is shared more times and faster than less violent content on Twitter, and if different online communities can be characterized by the level of violence of the visual content they share. The results show that the level of violence in an image tweet does not correlate with the number of retweets and mentions it receives that the time to retweet is marginally lower for image tweets containing a high level of violence and that the level of violence in image tweets differs between communities.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Rossi, Luca","Neumayer, Christina","Henrichsen, Jesper","Beck, Lucas K."],"cuid":["S02-100.00-038250"],"filters":{"added":"2023-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Digital media","Image recognition","Political participation","Political science"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"measuring violence a computational analysis of violence and propagation of image tweets from political protest","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101333721/SSRN-id3938168.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-038250","identifier":[{"id":"85124494913","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-02a52d4f-8d8c-440a-ba90-b45237b9afae","ku-7bd28049-5c3f-4237-8c2f-a8a6962ae47c"],"keyword":["digital media","image recognition","political participation","political science"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-9E7CA9467446F2A5DCDD7FC7754CBFB0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/Delivery.cfm/SSRN_ID3938168_code1587676.pdf?abstractid=3938168&mirid=1&type=2","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=3938168","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://papers.ssrn.com/abstract=3938168","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101333721/SSRN-id3938168.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/408302975/ssrn_3938168.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human Data Interaction Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Humanities, Department of Communication"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Christina","key":"neumayerpau","last":"Neumayer","locid":"15f1c085-b93f-40cc-9ef6-b8ae24783438","name":"Neumayer, Christina","orcid":"0000-0003-0450-2983","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jesper","key":"henrichsenpau","last":"Henrichsen","name":"Henrichsen, Jesper","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Lucas K.","key":"beckpau","last":"Beck","name":"Beck, Lucas K.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/08944393211055429","issn":["08944393","15528286"],"issn_org":["08944393"],"issue":"3","journal":"Social Science Computer Review","pages":"905-925","status":"p","title":"Social Science Computer Review","volume":"41"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85124494913"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-02a52d4f-8d8c-440a-ba90-b45237b9afae","ku-7bd28049-5c3f-4237-8c2f-a8a6962ae47c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Measuring Violence : A Computational Analysis of Violence and Propagation of Image Tweets From Political Protest","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-038250","year":2023,"yearsub":2023}
{"abstract":"This paper contributes to the ongoing effort to describe and quantify the quality of information that is shared on large social media platforms. We do this by complementing existing research that provided a first quantitative assessment of the quality of the information circulating on Facebook among US users. Leveraging an updated version of the same data source — Meta's URL Shares Dataset — and replicating much of the methodology, we quantify the trustworthy and untrustworthy links to external websites that have been shared on Facebook in the period between 2019 and 2022 in three major European countries (Germany, France, and Italy). We observe a clear decline in the number of URLs present in the dataset and an increase in the URLs from untrustworthy domains as a percentage of the total URLs shared in a year. This increase seems to be higher in electoral years (in Germany and in Italy) but it does not translate into an increase of Views received from untrustworthy sources.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Rossi, Luca","Giglietto, Fabio","Marino, Giada"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718919"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Italy"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Universita' degli Studi de Urbino Carlo Bo"],"keyword":["Facebook","Trustworthy news sources","URL Shares Dataset"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"cracking open the european newsfeed","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.51685/jqd.2023.020","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-718919","ids":["itu-d00a3e04-2735-47c3-9619-75a2db49bc1e"],"keyword":["Facebook","Trustworthy news sources","URL Shares Dataset"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-1254B2C23F8613BCCAF43AB57C002FDE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.51685/jqd.2023.020","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"rem","url":"https://journalqd.org/article/view/4377/3604","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human Data Interaction Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"it","key":"universita degli studi de urbino carlo bo","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universita' degli Studi de Urbino Carlo Bo","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Fabio","key":"gigliettopau","last":"Giglietto","name":"Giglietto, Fabio","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Giada","key":"marinopau","last":"Marino","name":"Marino, Giada","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"]},"sid":["itu-d00a3e04-2735-47c3-9619-75a2db49bc1e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Cracking Open the European Newsfeed","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718919","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"This paper analyzes the literature on problematic COVID-19 information published at the onset of the pandemic in 2020. It explores how scientific research has addressed this issue from a disciplinary, methodological and substantive perspective in different world regions. Three hundred seventy-eight articles were analyzed using content analysis and computational methods, including social network analysis and text mining. The study revealed a multidisciplinary field characterized by substantial contributions from medicine and social sciences and with a certain degree of interdisciplinarity and international collaborations. Research devoted particular attention to infodemic and conspiracy theories and their impact on compliance with health-protective behaviors, and showed a general preference for quantitative methods such as surveys. Most contributions focused on European and Americas regions and were from authors working in the same areas. Attention to various topics was also geographically differentiated. For example, conspiracy theories and informational factors that may influence COVID-compliant behaviors characterize the whole corpus, but Western research did the lion’s share. Similarly, the attention paid to different social media platforms differed geographically. Worldwide attention was dedicated to global social media platforms such as Twitter, WhatsApp, Facebook and Instagram. In contrast, geographically specific platforms (e.g., VKontakte or WeChat) have been studied less and mainly in regions where they are used the most. Focusing on a crucial period of the COVID-19 infodemic such as the first year of the pandemic, this study contributes to the literature on problematic information by highlighting how misinformation and disinformation research was carried out within and at the crossroads of disciplines and geographic regions. It suggests under-explored areas by contrast with the most relevant trends and establishes a foundation for benchmarking with post-2020 research on this topic.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Righetti, Nicola","Rossi, Luca","Marino, Giada"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382302"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Austria","Italy"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Sassari","University of Vienna"],"keyword":["Covid 19","Disinformation","Infodemic","Misinformation","Review"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"at the onset of an infodemic geographic and disciplinary boundaries in researching problematic covid 000000000019 information","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/100511687/View_of_At_the_onset_of_an_infodemic_Geographic_and_disciplinary_boundaries_in_researching_problematic_COVID_19_information_First_Monday.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382302","identifier":[{"id":"85160051025","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-45a78a69-53c2-4f63-9318-e630a57234c7"],"keyword":["COVID-19","Disinformation","Infodemic","Misinformation","Review"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-1A944D04B49DECD918CFB3C4B1EA6981"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"rem","url":"https://journals.uic.edu/ojs/index.php/fm/article/view/12557","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/100511687/View_of_At_the_onset_of_an_infodemic_Geographic_and_disciplinary_boundaries_in_researching_problematic_COVID_19_information_First_Monday.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"at","key":"university of vienna","loc":["Austria","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Vienna","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human Data Interaction Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"it","key":"university of sassari","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Sassari","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nicola","key":"righettipau","last":"Righetti","name":"Righetti, Nicola","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Giada","key":"marinopau","last":"Marino","name":"Marino, Giada","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["13960458","13960466"],"issn_org":["13960466"],"issue":"7","journal":"First Monday","publisher":"First Monday Editorial Group","status":"p","title":"First Monday","volume":"27"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85160051025"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-45a78a69-53c2-4f63-9318-e630a57234c7"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"At the onset of an infodemic: Geographic and disciplinary boundaries in researching problematic COVID-19 information","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382302","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"A search with minimal model dependence for physics beyond the Standard Model in events featuring three or four charged leptons (3ℓ and 4ℓ, ℓ=e,μ) is presented. The analysis aims to be sensitive to a wide range of potential new-physics theories simultaneously. This analysis uses data from pp collisions delivered by the Large Hadron Collider at a centre-of-mass energy of s=13 TeV and recorded with the ATLAS detector, corresponding to the full Run 2 dataset of 139 fb−1. The 3ℓ and 4ℓ phase space is divided into 22 event categories according to the number of leptons in the event, the missing transverse momentum, the invariant mass of the leptons, and the presence of leptons originating from a Z-boson candidate. These event categories are analysed independently for the presence of deviations from the Standard Model. No statistically significant deviations from the Standard Model predictions are observed. Upper limits for all signal regions are reported in terms of the visible cross-section.","author_count":2825,"contributors":["Aad, G.","Abbott, B.","Abbott, D. C.","Abed Abud, A.","Abeling, K.","Abhayasinghe, D. K.","Abidi, S. H.","AbouZeid, Ossama Sherif Alexander","Abramowicz, H.","Abreu, H.","Abulaiti, Y.","Abusleme Hoffman, A. C.","Acharya, B. S.","Achkar, B.","Adam, L.","Adam Bourdarios, C.","Adamczyk, L.","Adamek, L.","Adelman, J.","Adiguzel, A.","Adorni, S.","Adye, T.","Affolder, A. A.","Afik, Y.","Agapopoulou, C.","Agaras, M. N.","Agarwala, J.","Aggarwal, A.","Agheorghiesei, C.","Aguilar-Saavedra, J. A.","Ahmad, A.","Ahmadov, F.","Ahmed, W. S.","Ai, X.","Aielli, G.","Akatsuka, S.","Akbiyik, M.","Åkesson, T. P.A.","Akimov, A. V.","Al Khoury, K.","Alberghi, G. L.","Albert, J.","Alconada Verzini, M. J.","Alderweireldt, S.","Aleksa, M.","Aleksandrov, I. N.","Alexa, C.","Alexopoulos, T.","Alfonsi, A.","Alfonsi, F.","Alhroob, M.","Ali, B.","Ali, S.","Aliev, M.","Alimonti, G.","Allaire, C.","Allbrooke, B. M.M.","Allport, P. P.","Aloisio, A.","Alonso, F.","A