{"abstract":"This chapter develops an argument for acknowledging the ways in which computer vision sees the world when introducing machine learning techniques into the study of images of conflict. Reflecting on a process of training a computer vision system to see violence in Twitter images from protests, we suggest a conceptual framework for tracing how computer vision learns to see. Our conceptual understanding operates within three dimensions: (a) technology and problems of current computer vision solutions when working with a socio-culturally complex phenomenon such as the visual representation of conflict; (b) epistemology at intersection of humanistic and sociological inquiry, and computer vision; and (c) humans both as subject of the computer vision as well as often invisible actors involved in the training of a computer vision system. Tracing the process of how computer vision learns to see within the dimensions of technology, epistemology and humans reminds us to avoid oversimplification and classifications in favour of quantification. Instead, this conceptual framework encourages us to produce meaning by unfolding the complexity of computer vision’s ways of seeing images from conflict.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Neumayer, Christina","Rossi, Luca"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-409782"],"filters":{"added":"2022-11","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"seeing images from conflict through computer vision technology epistemology and humans","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-409782","ids":["ku-1b64db81-3808-44ff-a1ff-2802ee4130b2"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-10E2094FE1A1AACD3A2CA4A5C892EA8D"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Humanities, Department of Communication"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Christina","key":"neumayerpau","last":"Neumayer","locid":"15f1c085-b93f-40cc-9ef6-b8ae24783438","name":"Neumayer, Christina","orcid":"0000-0003-0450-2983","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","name":"Rossi, Luca","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Mette","key":"mortensenpedh","last":"Mortensen","name":"Mortensen, Mette","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Ally","key":"mccrowyoungpedh","last":"McCrow-Young","name":"McCrow-Young, Ally","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4324/9781003176923-9","publisher":"Routledge","status":"p","title":"Social Media Images and Conflicts"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-1b64db81-3808-44ff-a1ff-2802ee4130b2"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Seeing Images from Conflict through Computer Vision : Technology, Epistemology and Humans","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-409782","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Social researchers often walk a tightrope. On the one hand, they try to adhere to ethical principles when working with vulnerable groups. On the other, they aim to not cast these groups as inherently lacking agency. Digital practices have only made this dilemma more acute by strengthening the agency of vulnerable groups—in this case, those within the European border regime threatened by deportation—while also putting migrants at increased danger from bordering and surveillance practices. Drawing from a militant research approach, as well as from the Autonomy of Migration (AoM) perspective, this contribution illuminates this dilemma, while also arguing for reflexive and contextualised ethics aimed towards solidarity and social change.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Fischer, Leandros","Jørgensen, Martin Bak"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-057268"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"impossible research ethical challenges in the digital study of deportable populations within the european border regime","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-81226-3_6","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-057268","ids":["aau-18a89522-de04-4380-8432-44652ecfe81f"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-CB4C1F2C8BBBD55E55BF780F66AD6A05"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-81226-3_6","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Culture and Learning","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Culture and Learning, Democracy, Migration and Society (DEMOS)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leandros","key":"fischerpau","last":"Fischer","locid":"1b27631f-9077-49e5-9049-6ad03ea7acfa","name":"Fischer, Leandros","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Martin Bak","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"5e41f079-bbd2-4a57-a1d3-8fad8c9233de","name":"Jørgensen, Martin Bak","orcid":"0000-0003-0104-8543","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Marie","key":"sandbergpedh","last":"Sandberg","name":"Sandberg, Marie","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Luca","key":"rossipedh","last":"Rossi","name":"Rossi, Luca","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Vasilis","key":"galispedh","last":"Galis","name":"Galis, Vasilis","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Martin","key":"bakjørgensenpedh","last":"Bak Jørgensen","name":"Bak Jørgensen, Martin","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-81226-3_6","eisbn":["N 9783030812263"],"isbn":["9783030812256"],"pages":"141-166","place":"Cham, Switzerland","publisher":"Palgrave Macmillan","series":"Approaches to Social Inequality and Difference","status":"p","subtitle":"Caring For (Big) Data?","title":"Research Methodologies and Ethical Challenges in Digital Migration Studies"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-18a89522-de04-4380-8432-44652ecfe81f"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Impossible Research? Ethical Challenges in the (Digital) Study of Deportable Populations Within the European Border Regime","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-057268","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"A multiplex network models different modes of interaction among same-type entities. In this article, we provide a taxonomy of community detection algorithms in multiplex networks. We characterize the different algorithms based on various properties and we discuss the type of communities detected by each method. We then provide an extensive experimental evaluation of the reviewed methods to answer three main questions: to what extent the evaluated methods are able to detect ground-truth communities, to what extent different methods produce similar community structures, and to what extent the evaluated methods are scalable. One goal of this survey is to help scholars and practitioners to choose the right methods for the data and the task at hand, while also emphasizing when such choice is problematic.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Magnani, Matteo","Hanteer, Obaida","Interdonato, Roberto","Rossi, Luca","Tagarelli, Andrea"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384651"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["France","Italy","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","CIRAD, UMR TETIS,","University of Calabria","Uppsala University"],"keyword":["Community detection","Experimental evaluation","Ground truth communities","Multiplex networks","Taxonomy of algorithms"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"community detection in multiplex networks","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85950596/1910.07646.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384651","identifier":[{"id":"85108091175","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-