evasion","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-484901","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"This OA book investigates the methodological and ethical dilemmas involved when working with digital technologies and large-scale datasets in relation to ethnographic studies of digital migration practices and trajectories. Digital technologies reshape not only every phase of the migration process itself (by providing new ways to access, to share and preserve relevant information) but also the activities of other actors, from solidarity networks to border control agencies. In doing so, digital technologies create a whole new set of ethical and methodological challenges for migration studies: from data access to data interpretation, privacy protection, and research ethics more generally. Of specific concern are the aspects of digital migration researchers accessing digital platforms used by migrants, who are subject to precarious and insecure life circumstances, lack recognised papers and are in danger of being rejected and deported. Thus, the authors call for new modes of caring for (big) data when researching migrants’ digital practices in the configuration of migration and borders. Besides taking proper care of research participants’ privacy, autonomy, and security, this also spans carefully establishing analytically sustainable environments for the respective data sets. In doing so, the book argues that it is essential to carefully reflect on researchers’ own positioning as being part of the challenge they seek to address.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Sandberg, Marie","Rossi, Luca","Galis, Vasilis","Jørgensen, Martin Bak"],"cuid":["S02-096.36-012119"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Big data","Data Sets","Digital Humanities","Digital technologies","Ethnography","Migration","Open Access","Privacy","Research ethics","Social media"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci","soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"research methodologies and ethical challenges in digital migration studies caring for big data","src":["aau","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"db","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-81226-3","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-096.36-012119","ids":["aau-febce72d-515c-452f-a483-c29ad603f3aa","itu-cc3f5eef-65d4-49c9-b0f8-dea0afe853a0"],"keyword":["big data","data sets","digital humanities","digital technologies","ethnography","migration","open access","privacy","research ethics","social media"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology","Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-6-9-965E2AA65ECD1C1A8EA933E2E4E62E3D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-030-81226-3","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-81226-3","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human Data Interaction Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Culture and Learning","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Culture and Learning, Democracy, Migration and Society (DEMOS)","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Politics and Society, MIX - Center for Displacement, Migration and Integration"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marie","key":"sandbergped","last":"Sandberg","name":"Sandberg, Marie","rank":1,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossiped","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":2,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Vasilis","key":"galisped","last":"Galis","locid":"443362d7-b255-460d-a559-0fa80a8d9fbc","name":"Galis, Vasilis","orcid":"0000-0002-4621-3699","rank":3,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Martin Bak","key":"jørgensenped","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"5e41f079-bbd2-4a57-a1d3-8fad8c9233de","name":"Jørgensen, Martin Bak","orcid":"0000-0003-0104-8543","rank":4,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-81226-3","eisbn":["N 9783030812263"],"isbn":["9783030812256"],"pages":"269","place":"Cham, Switzerland","publisher":"Palgrave Macmillan","series":"Approaches to Social Inequality and Difference","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-febce72d-515c-452f-a483-c29ad603f3aa","itu-cc3f5eef-65d4-49c9-b0f8-dea0afe853a0"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Research Methodologies and Ethical Challenges in Digital Migration Studies : Caring For (Big) Data?","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-096.36-012119","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"The field of digital methods has emerged over the past two decades at the interface between media studies, science and technology studies (STS), computer science, and information design (Marres 2017; Rogers 2013). The core questions and ambitions covered closely resemble the kind of digital migration studies presented in this book. First, a commitment to studying wider sociocultural phenomena on, through, and with digital traces from online media. Second, a desire to critically engage with the role of these media, their platforms, and algorithms as infrastructures of social life. Third, a curiosity about how to wrongfoot entrenched divides between offline and online, actual and virtual, or qualitative and quantitative. And finally, ongoing experimentation with computational techniques for large-scale data capture analysis in conjunction with ethnographic approaches to deep sensemaking and interpretation. Indeed, to begin","author_count":5,"contributors":["Munk, Anders Kristian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-122725"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Big data","CARE","Critical proximity","Digital Methods","Gephi"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"caring as critical proximity a call for toolmaking in digital migration studies","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/handle/20.500.12657/51964/978-3-030-81226-3.pdf?sequence=1#page=243","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-122725","ids":["aau-fa31386a-382b-49e6-9f91-d62ae7f9254c"],"keyword":["Big data","Care","Critical proximity","Digital methods","Gephi","Navicrawler"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-3A39B0F6A627D68C04372A815BE49B69"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/handle/20.500.12657/51964/978-3-030-81226-3.pdf?sequence=1#page=243","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-81226-3_10","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Culture and Learning","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Culture and Learning, The Techno-Anthropology Lab","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, AI for the People"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anders Kristian","key":"munkpau","last":"Munk","locid":"1152f3e1-f7da-4ced-8a78-b16a32675dca","name":"Munk, Anders Kristian","orcid":"0000-0002-5542-3065","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Marie","key":"sandbergpedh","last":"Sandberg","name":"Sandberg, Marie","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Luca","key":"rossipedh","last":"Rossi","name":"Rossi, Luca","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Vasilis","key":"galispedh","last":"Galis","name":"Galis, Vasilis","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Martin Bak","key":"jørgensenpedh","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Martin Bak","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9783030812263"],"isbn":["9783030812256"],"pages":"235-245","publisher":"Palgrave Macmillan","status":"p","subtitle":"Caring For (Big) Data?","title":"Research Methodologies and Ethical Challenges in Digital Migration Studies"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-fa31386a-382b-49e6-9f91-d62ae7f9254c"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Caring as Critical Proximity : A Call for Toolmaking in Digital Migration Studies","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-122725","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"In this article, we reconstruct the academic discourse surrounding social media and elections in an Italian context. We follow Neumayer and Rossi’s conceptualization of academic discourse concerning political protest and digital technology as constructed out of three components: (a) the social phenomena under investigation, (b) technological development, and (c) methods and techniques. In the context of social media and elections, these three components may be identified as (a) the research questions that researchers seek to answer, (b) the social media platforms and data used for the analysis, and (c) the methods adopted to analyze the data. While these three dimensions are deeply intertwined, we argue that, when analyzed independently, it is possible to better see both the longitudinal evolution of each dimension and their interdependencies.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Rossi, Luca","Righetti, Nicola","Marino, Giada"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384779"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Austria","Italy"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Universitat Wien","University of Sassari"],"keyword":["Elections","Italy","Methods","Social media"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"nearly ten years of social media and political elections in italy questions platforms and methods","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/20563051211063460","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384779","identifier":[{"id":"85120864853","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ee67d620-3bee-47da-a8bf-17c521fa79d0"],"keyword":["Italy","elections","methods","social media"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-461B5CAB10D177176C707A601A5DAAA8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/20563051211063460","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86398136/20563051211063460.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human Data Interaction Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"at","key":"universitat wien","loc":["Austria","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universitat Wien","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"it","key":"university of sassari","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Sassari","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nicola","key":"righettipau","last":"Righetti","name":"Righetti, Nicola","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Giada","key":"marinopau","last":"Marino","name":"Marino, Giada","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/20563051211063460","issn":["20563051"],"issue":"4","journal":"Social Media + Society","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"Social Media + Society","volume":"7"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85120864853"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ee67d620-3bee-47da-a8bf-17c521fa79d0"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"(Nearly) Ten Years of Social Media and Political Elections in Italy: Questions, Platforms, and Methods","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384779","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"A humorous photograph of a man holding up a sign and walking through the protests against the G20 summit in Hamburg is one of the most shared tweets posted during the anti-capitalist Welcome to Hell demonstration taking place on 6 July 2017. Instead of a protest slogan, however, the handwritten sign says: “I am a local resident, and I am just going to Edeka [German supermarket chain]”. Various photographs of the man taken in different places are shared prominently in social media feeding into the narrative of the G20 protests. This is only one of the various images and videos from the protests that humorously juxtapose everyday activities with protest scenes such as burning barricades as well as police in full gear. While previous research has shown that activists, police and institutional media alike share their perspectives from political protests in social media (Neumayer, Rossi & Karlsson 2016; Neumayer & Rossi 2018), these seemingly neutral perspectives on the actual conflict have received relatively little attention (e.g., Jensen, Neumayer & Rossi 2018). In this research we ask which role such images play for the representation of political protest in social media. To do so, we collected tweets (n=678946) based on protest hashtags from the G20 demonstrations in Hamburg in the time period 6-8 July 2017. With a social network analysis to detect communities in the data, we find that while we can detect communities around user accounts of activist collectives, media institutions, and authorities (such as the Hamburg police), we cannot observe a network centred around user accounts of residents of Hamburg. As seemingly neutral humorous perspectives which do neither take the side of the activists or the authorities, these images seem to be shared across various communities in the network. Yet, these are among the most frequently shared tweets based on number of retweets and mentions. Through a narrative analysis of the most frequently shared tweets with a particular focus on such humorous juxtapositions of the protests and everyday activities of residents, we argue that these seemingly apolitical images and videos shared across communities, still play a disruptive role in the narrative about political protest. The privileging of humorous content (that users are perhaps more inclined to share in social media than political messages) over political action or violent imagery, might (while not actively taking sides) push activists’ grievances in the background and make their actions appear as disruptive. With these findings, this research contributes to our understanding of how the logics of social media might influence narratives of contemporary protests.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Neumayer, Christina","Rossi, Luca","Jensen, Minna Soltani"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-520560"],"event":{"end":"2021-09-09","place":"Braga, pt","start":"2021-09-06","title":"ECREA 2021","year":"2021"},"filters":{"added":"2022-04","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"disruptive humour the juxtaposition of mass protest and everyday activities in social media images","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-520560","ids":["ku-d4c05c98-8ec6-41dc-937d-7935d8e5349b"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Local-1-1-79583B95ACE96CCA8A2F1F7D2063F00B"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Humanities, Department of Communication"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Christina","key":"neumayerpau","last":"Neumayer","locid":"15f1c085-b93f-40cc-9ef6-b8ae24783438","name":"Neumayer, Christina","orcid":"0000-0003-0450-2983","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","name":"Rossi, Luca","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Minna Soltani","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","name":"Jensen, Minna Soltani","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-d4c05c98-8ec6-41dc-937d-7935d8e5349b"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Disruptive humour : The juxtaposition of mass protest and everyday activities in social media images","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-520560","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Social media data are increasingly used to study a variety of social phenomena. This development is based on the assumption that digital traces left on social media can provide insights into the nature of human interaction. In this research, we turn our attention to what remains invisible in research based on social media data. Using Andrea Brighenti’s work on ‘social visibility’ as a point of departure, we unpack data invisibilities, as they are created within four dimensions: people and intentionality, technologies and tools, accessibility and form, and meaning and imaginaries. We introduce the notion of quasi-visible data as an intermediary between visible and invisible data highlighting the processual character of data invisibilities. With this conceptual framework we contribute to developing a more reflective and ethical field of research into the study of social phenomena based on social media data. We conclude by arguing that distancing ourselves from the assumption that all social media data are visible and focusing on the invisible will enhance our understanding of digital data.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Neumayer, Christina","Rossi, Luca","Struthers, David M."],"cuid":["S02-093.23-038290"],"filters":{"added":"2022-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Computational methods","Digital Methods","Epistemology","Social media data","Visibilities"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"invisible data a framework for understanding visibility processes in social media data","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/2056305120984472","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-093.23-038290","identifier":[{"id":"85099964390","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-42a79dae-2d70-4cbc-a0f2-9f67f164980e","ku-77db0bd5-785b-46ac-a7f5-59c1f1425430"],"keyword":["computational methods","digital methods","epistemology","social media data","visibilities"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-825ECF848F330B4485BAED26AF0035C7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/301369620/neumayer2056305120984472.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/2056305120984472","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86232931/Invisible_Data.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Humanities, Department of Communication"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human Data Interaction Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Christina","key":"neumayerpau","last":"Neumayer","locid":"15f1c085-b93f-40cc-9ef6-b8ae24783438","name":"Neumayer, Christina","orcid":"0000-0003-0450-2983","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"David","key":"strutherspau","last":"Struthers","name":"Struthers, David","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/2056305120984472","issn":["20563051"],"issue":"1","journal":"Social Media + Society","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"Social Media + Society","volume":"7"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85099964390"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-42a79dae-2d70-4cbc-a0f2-9f67f164980e","ku-77db0bd5-785b-46ac-a7f5-59c1f1425430"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Invisible Data: A Framework for Understanding Visibility Processes in Social Media Data","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-093.23-038290","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"We present a methodology integrating social media data, data from qualitative research and network analysis. Qualitative insights gained from ethnographic fieldwork are used to collect and annotate social network data, and social media data is used as part of the ethnography to identify relevant actors and topics. The methodology is presented in the context of an analysis of the Internet of Things in the European context.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Ustek-Spilda, Funda","Vega, Davide","Magnani, Matteo","Rossi, Luca","Shklovski, Irina","Lehuede, Sebastian","Powell, Alison"],"cuid":["S02-095.83-038332"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","London School of Economics","The London School of Economics and Political Science","University of Oxford","Uppsala University"],"keyword":["Data science","Ethnography","Internet of Things","Methodology","Triangulation","Twitter"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a twitter based study of the european internet of things","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10796-020-10008-5","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-095.83-038332","identifier":[{"id":"85085113532","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-78bf174c-516d-4288-8441-d237bc295648","ku-cfc3b507-dd7d-4d44-89f0-fb73463c751a"],"keyword":["Data science","Ethnography","Internet of Things","Methodology","Triangulation","Twitter"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],