{"author_count":7,"contributors":["Rossi, Luca","Orefice, Mario"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384134"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Italy"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Universita' degli Studi de Urbino Carlo Bo"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"comparing facebook and twitter during the 000000002013 general election in italy","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.routledge.com/products/9781138860766","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384134","ids":["itu-c3b3fa90-3bca-4f04-b14f-6fc9eec54221"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-679136A2525683ACB6F4F844A1D90F9D"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","The Department, Culture and Games"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"it","key":"universita degli studi de urbino carlo bo","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universita' degli Studi de Urbino Carlo Bo","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mario","key":"oreficepau","last":"Orefice","name":"Orefice, Mario","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Axel","key":"brunspedh","last":"Bruns","name":"Bruns, Axel","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Gunn","key":"enlipedh","last":"Enli","name":"Enli, Gunn","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Eli","key":"skogerbopedh","last":"Skogerbo","name":"Skogerbo, Eli","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Anders Olof","key":"larssonpedh","last":"Larsson","name":"Larsson, Anders Olof","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Christian","key":"christensenpedh","last":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Christian","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9781138860766"],"pages":"434-446","publisher":"Routledge","status":"p","title":"The Routledge Companion to Social Media and Politics"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84967333820"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-c3b3fa90-3bca-4f04-b14f-6fc9eec54221"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Comparing Facebook and Twitter during the 2013 General Election in Italy","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384134","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"In this paper, we describe the results of a qualitative study conducted with 120 Italian Facebook users to investigate how Facebook enables people to achieve a mutually constitutive intimacy with their own friendship network: a negotiation of intimacy in public through self-disclosure, where the affordances of the platform are useful to elicit significant reactions, validations and demonstrations of affection from others. We observed that, in order to achieve various levels of intimacy on Facebook, people engage in various strategies: Showing rather than telling, Sharing implicit content, Tagging, Expectation of mutual understanding and Liking. These strategies produce a collaborative disclosure that relies on others’ cooperation to maintain the boundaries between private and public space. Based on these premises, we developed a framework of collaborative strategies for managing public intimacy that both systematizes and extends the findings identified in previous studies of intimacy on Facebook. We describe this framework as networked intimacy and we discuss the consequences of it in the light of already existing research on online self-disclosure.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Farci, Manolo","Rossi, Luca","Boccia Artieri, Giovanni","Giglietto, Fabio"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384883"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Italy"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Universita' degli Studi de Urbino Carlo Bo"],"keyword":["Facebook","Intimacy","Self disclosure","Social network"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"networked intimacy intimacy and friendship among italian facebook users","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82451783/Networked_Intimacy.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384883","identifier":[{"id":"84976566310","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-f56097ab-92c6-4e81-93d9-9b78ec1088eb"],"keyword":["Facebook","Intimacy","Self-Disclosure","Social Network"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-8BED6A4637261FA6BD3559B40478CCE1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82451783/Networked_Intimacy.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"it","key":"universita degli studi de urbino carlo bo","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universita' degli Studi de Urbino Carlo Bo","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","The Department, Digital Society and Communication"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Manolo","key":"farcipau","last":"Farci","name":"Farci, Manolo","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Giovanni","key":"bocciaartieripau","last":"Boccia Artieri","name":"Boccia Artieri, Giovanni","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Fabio","key":"gigliettopau","last":"Giglietto","name":"Giglietto, Fabio","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/1369118x.2016.1203970","issn":["1369118X","14684462"],"issn_org":["1369118X"],"journal":"Information, Communication & Society","publisher":"Taylor & Francis","status":"p","title":"Information, Communication & Society"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84976566310"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f56097ab-92c6-4e81-93d9-9b78ec1088eb"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Networked Intimacy. Intimacy and Friendship among Italian Facebook users","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384883","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Main findings of the survey » Social media use is a daily practice in Denmark, however, frequency and type of use differ greatly. » Danes use social media primarily to read content; it is less frequently used for producing original content or for interacting with content produced by others. » Younger Danes are more active and present on social media platforms than older generations. The generation between 20 and 39 years is most likely to use Facebook in order to discuss politics with strangers. » When specifically looking at how users understand their communication on Facebook, it turns out that many of them view their communication as private. Especially the social network Facebook is used for private communication, e.g. exchanging messages with close friends and family. » In general, it is not very common for Danish citizens to actively engage in political debates online with strangers, and to change a personal view on a political issue after this. Nevertheless, there is a smaller group of Danes who say they do so.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Rossi, Luca","Schwartz, Sander Andreas","Mahnke, Martina Skrubbeltrang"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-038404"],"filters":{"added":"2022-04","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Content consumption","Generational differences","Online political engagement","Private communication","Social media usage"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"social media use amp political engagement in denmark report 000000002016","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://blogit.itu.dk/decidis/wp-content/uploads/sites/5/2016/03/Decidis_report_2016.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-038404","ids":["itu-e81b8c0c-1881-4956-9b92-9a0b32e24d8c","ku-c4c29a1b-4636-4424-9bdc-6d10cf84e6e3"],"keyword":["Content consumption","Generational differences","Online political engagement","Private communication","Social media usage"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-2-3623710FFD25D013BD90FCE5E6DB0D3B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://blogit.itu.dk/decidis/wp-content/uploads/sites/5/2016/03/Decidis_report_2016.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RUC Roskilde University","namevar":["Roskilde University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-RUC","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Humanities, Department of Communication"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Sander Andreas","key":"schwartzpau","last":"Schwartz","name":"Schwartz, Sander Andreas","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 1","first":"Martina Skrubbeltrang","key":"mahnkepau","last":"Mahnke","locid":"08dd116e-68b9-48f5-9d27-07e7a3a21983","name":"Mahnke, Martina Skrubbeltrang","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"5","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Roskilde University","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e81b8c0c-1881-4956-9b92-9a0b32e24d8c","ku-c4c29a1b-4636-4424-9bdc-6d10cf84e6e3"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Social Media Use &amp; Political engagement in Denmark : Report 2016","type":"Report","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-038404","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"This article investigates the relationship between the invention of new media technologies and scholarship concerning protest and political engagement. Building on an innovative approach that moves beyond a systematic literature review, this article contributes to our understanding of scholarship concerning digital communication technologies and how they may have been adopted and shaped protest movements and political engagement. Based on visualisations, we draw a sociotechnical timeline of protest and media technology scholarship within three dimensions: Technological development, methods and techniques, and the social phenomena under investigation. The article concludes by identifying major trends in protest and media technologies scholarship over the past 15 years. The sociotechnical timeline enhances our understanding of academic discourse at the intersection of protest and media technologies by highlighting shortcomings and potential for future research.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Neumayer, Christina","Rossi, Luca"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382041"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Academic Discourse","Collective action","Protest","Timeline","Visualisation","media technologies"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"000000000015 years of protest and media technologies scholarship a sociotechnical timeline","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/2056305116662180","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382041","identifier":[{"id":"85031414394","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-34bb1d58-6264-4a23-90ba-360f9901dd32"],"keyword":["academic discourse","collective action","media technologies","protest","timeline","visualisation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-8B88F62DD4A94A7A2637EE9B01ACF234"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/2056305116662180","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82342941/2056305116662180.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","The Department, Digital Society and Communication"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christina","key":"neumayerpau","last":"Neumayer","name":"Neumayer, Christina","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/2056305116662180","issn":["20563051"],"issue":"3","journal":"Social Media + Society","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"Social Media + Society","volume":"2"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85031414394"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-34bb1d58-6264-4a23-90ba-360f9901dd32"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"15 years of protest and media technologies scholarship: A sociotechnical timeline","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382041","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Despite the widely recognized centrality of images in contemporary activists communication empirical studies based on visual social media data are rare. This article addresses this challenge by analysing images (photos and videos) produced and propagated in the Blockupy Frankfurt protests against the opening of the European Central Bank (ECB) headquarters in Frankfurt am Main, Germany, on March 18, 2015. The article enhances our understanding of visual communication in protest and social media by exploring how different conflictual visual narratives produced by different actors emerge during the Bockupy Frankfurt protests on Twitter using event-specific hashtags (#Blockupy, #Destroika, #NoTroika). This research combines a content analysis of the most retweeted visual content (1%, N=119) with a narrative analysis of photos and videos. The article concludes by arguing that on Twitter, images of riots, peaceful protests, artistic action, as well as activists, police and news media struggle for public visibility. Different actors create parallel narratives representing a positive image of themselves by antagonising the other through visual narratives of physical and latent violence (particularly in images posted by the police Frankfurt am Main but also by activists) as well as (in the case of the activists) by a non-violent and colourful positive self-representation. Despite these non-violent narratives produced by activists we can observe a dominance of physical and latent violence in reporting about protest events through visual content on Twitter.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Neumayer, Christina","Rossi, Luca","Vulpuis, Julie"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384663"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Content analysis","Narrative analysis","Protest movements","Social media","Visual communication"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"images of protest in contested social media production propagation and narratives","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://journals.uic.edu/ojs/index.php/spir/article/view/8643/6886","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384663","ids":["itu-e65a661d-b43c-409e-94ab-b3610a3948be"],"keyword":["Content Analysis","Narrative Analysis","Protest Movements","Social Media","Visual Communication"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-12C4F7645DBEB9260941D6800CCCAA71"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://journals.uic.edu/ojs/index.php/spir/article/view/8643/6886","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christina","key":"neumayerpau","last":"Neumayer","name":"Neumayer, Christina","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Julie","key":"vulpuispau","last":"Vulpuis","name":"Vulpuis, Julie","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"place":"Berlin","status":"p","subtitle":"The 17th Annual Conference of the Association of Internet Researchers","title":"AoIR Selected Paper of Internet Research 2016","volume":"6"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e65a661d-b43c-409e-94ab-b3610a3948be"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Images of protest in contested social media: Production, propagation, and narratives","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384663","year":"2016"}
{"abstract":"The Large Observatory For x-ray Timing (LOFT) is a mission concept which was proposed to ESA as M3 and M4 candidate in the framework of the Cosmic Vision 2015-2025 program. Thanks to the unprecedented combination of effective area and spectral resolution of its main instrument and the uniquely large field of view of its wide field monitor, LOFT will be able to study the behaviour of matter in extreme conditions such as the strong gravitational field in the innermost regions close to black holes and neutron stars and the supra-nuclear densities in the interiors of neutron stars. The science payload is based on a Large Area Detector (LAD, >8m2 effective area, 2-30 keV, 240 eV spectral resolution, 1 degree collimated field of view) and a Wide Field Monitor (WFM, 2-50 keV, 4 steradian field of view, 1 arcmin source location accuracy, 300 eV spectral resolution). The WFM is equipped with an on-board system for bright events (e.g., GRB) localization. The trigger time and position of these events are broadcast to the ground within 30 s from discovery. In this paper we present the current technical and programmatic status of the mission. ...","author_count":437,"contributors":["Feroci, M.","Bozzo, E.","Hernanz, M.","van der Klis, M.","Orleanski, P.","Pohl, M.","Santangelo, A.","Schanne, S.","Stella, L.","Takahashi, T.","Tamura, H.","Watts, A.","Wilms, J.","Zane, S.","Bhattacharyya, S.","Agudo, I.","Ahangarianabhari, M.","Albertus, C.","Alford, M.","Alpar, A.","Altamirano, D.","Alvarez, L.","Amati, L.","Amoros, C.","Antonelli, A.","Argan, A.","Artigue, R.","Artigues, B.","Atteia, J. -L.","Azzarello, P.","Bakala, P.","Ballantyne, D.","Baldazzi, G.","Baldo, M.","Balman, S.","Barbera, M.","van Baren, C.","Barret, D.","Baykal, A.","Begelman, M.","Behar, E.","Behar, O.","Belloni, T.","Bernardini, F.","Bertuccio, G.","Bianchi, S.","Bianchini, A.","Binko, P.","Blay, P.","Bocchino, F.","Bode, M.","Bodin, P.","Bombaci, I.","Bonnet Bidaud, J. -M.","Boutloukos, S.","Bouyjo