{"abstract":"The shadow of Big Data: data-citizenship and exclusion Big data are understood as being able to provide insights on human behaviour at an individual as well as at an aggregated societal level (Manyka et al. 2011). These insights are expected to be more detailed and precise than anything before thanks to the large volume of digital data and to the unobstrusive nature of the data collection (Fishleigh 2014). Within this perspective, these two dimensions (volume and unobstrusiveness) define contemporary big data techniques as a socio-technical offering to society, a live representation of itself. More precisely, within a system theory approach (Luhmann 1982, 2010), large-scale big data work as a boundary-drawing operation where the difference between the system and its environment is reproduced. Here the result of system observation is defined by what is made visible as (big) data. Within this process \"data-citizenship\" emerges. Data-citizenship assumes that citizens will be visible to the state through the data they produce. On a general level data-citizenship shifts citizenship from an intrinsic status of a group of people to a status achieved through action. This approach assumes equal possibilities of action for every citizen, even if research has shown that an unequal distribution of participatory potential is unavoidable (boyd & Crawford 2012). Data-citizenships echoes what was envisioned by Luhmann (2010): When society is defined through the metacode of inclusion/exclusion, persons (of which citizens are equivalent within the legal system) are emancipated depending on their ability of being data producers. We will present preliminary results of an ongoing research about the digitalisation process in the Danish public sector. Through the analysis of specific examples we will show how those citizens who do not leave (digital) traces not only appear at the margins or entirely disappear (Bourdieu 1999) in the shadow of big-data.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Rossi, Luca","Hjelholt, Morten","Neumayer, Christina"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383362"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["social systems big data"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the shadow of big data data citizenship and exclusion","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-383362","ids":["itu-8e6deaf9-0fec-4f5b-aad2-8731d82d796a"],"keyword":["social systems, big data,"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-D47C643E26F33E7111CA995DC0E5F8B9"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","The Department, Culture and Games","The Department, Digital Society and Communication"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten","key":"hjelholtpau","last":"Hjelholt","locid":"10e4fc8e-556f-4104-b744-409caedc2276","name":"Hjelholt, Morten","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christina","key":"neumayerpau","last":"Neumayer","name":"Neumayer, Christina","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-8e6deaf9-0fec-4f5b-aad2-8731d82d796a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Shadow of Big Data: Data-Citizenship and Exclusion","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383362","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Multilayer networks, in particular multilayer social networks, where users belong to and interact on different networks at the same time, are an active research area in social network analysis, computer science, and physics. These networks have traditionally been studied within these separate research communities, leading to the development of several independent models and methods to deal with the same set of problems. This book unifies and consolidates existing practical and theoretical knowledge on multilayer networks including data collection and analysis, modeling, and mining of multilayer social network systems, the evolution of interconnected social networks, and dynamic processes such as information spreading. A single real dataset is used to illustrate the concepts presented throughout the book, demonstrating both the practical utility and the potential shortcomings of the various methods. Researchers from all areas of network analysis will learn new aspects and future directions of this emerging field.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Dickison, Mark","Magnani, Matteo","Rossi, Luca"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382825"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Uppsala University"],"keyword":["Network analysis","social network analysis book"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"multilayer social networks","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"db","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85006811036","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382825","identifier":[{"id":"85006811036","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-677dc18f-488d-4be6-b0de-a9f9e0f65f40"],"keyword":["network analysis","social network analysis book"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-A0B1554E939317B680895C9C9D0C6A71"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"se","key":"uppsala university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Uppsala University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","The Department, Digital Society and Communication"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Mark","key":"dickisonpau","last":"Dickison","name":"Dickison, Mark","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Matteo","key":"magnanipau","last":"Magnani","name":"Magnani, Matteo","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9781107438750"],"pages":"203","place":"New York","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-677dc18f-488d-4be6-b0de-a9f9e0f65f40"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Multilayer Social Networks","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382825","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"This chapters provide methods to describe in a quantitative way, a social network site as a whole. Besides the most commonly used metrics such as number of users or level of activity the chapter proposes more complex metrics based on topi diversity and language fidelity.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Rossi, Luca","Di Lascio, F Marta L","Pacelli, Barbara","Magnani, Matteo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384562"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Italy","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Libera Università di Bolzano","Uppsala University"],"keyword":["Language Fidelity","Quantitative Metrics","Social network analysis","Topic Diversity","User Activity"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"descrivere un social network site un approccio empirico","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"http://www.francoangeli.it/ricerca/Scheda_libro.aspx?ID=20916","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384562","ids":["itu-e0259a09-0930-4271-8f5f-6ae3e7a57b14"],"keyword":["Language Fidelity","Quantitative Metrics","Social Network Analysis","Topic Diversity","User Activity"],"lang":"Italian","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-6DA361966070B87C15421E5EE33F23B8"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","The Department, Culture and Games"],"nora_co